{"chunk_id":"71d7fe","wall_time_seconds":1.733701791,"exit_code":0,"original_token_count":3924,"output":"{"body":"A squash-merged branch shows `⊂` with its row dimmed, and `+305 -42` in `main…±` immediately beside it. Both are right: `⊂` is a content check against main's tip, `main…±` is the three-dot diff from the merge-base, and a squash merge doesn't move the merge-base. Read together they contradict each other, and the bright counts carry further than the dim symbol.\n\nThe idea: measure `main…±` as what merging would add rather than what the branch wrote, i.e. `git diff <target-tree> <tree from merge-tree --write-tree target branch>`. That is the quantity the integration check already computes as a boolean (`merge_adds_nothing`), so the column and the symbol beside it would be reading the same thing.\n\n## Performance\n\n`merge-tree` already runs on every row. `Status` is a default column, its `required_tasks()` include `MergeTreeConflicts` and `WouldMergeAdd`, the in-memory `merge_tree` map collapses both to a single subprocess, and `MergeTreeOutcome::Clean` already carries the resulting tree SHA. So the added operation is one tree-to-tree diff, and it replaces the three-dot diff rather than adding to it.\n\n| repo | tree-to-tree (proposed) | three-dot (today) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 259 commits | 11.2 ms ± 0.5 | 10.9 ms ± 0.4 |\n| git.git, 81,908 commits | 11.8 ms ± 0.3 | 11.5 ms ± 1.4 |\n\nFor scale, a cold `wt list` over 17 worktrees measured 390 ms against ~160 ms warm, so the entire cold git budget is ~230 ms and this shifts nothing within it.\n\n## Caching\n\nCache the `LineDiff`, never the tree SHA. `merge-tree --write-tree` writes objects that nothing references, so a persisted tree SHA can be pruned by `git gc` and a later read would fail; the computed stats carry no such dependency.\n\nThe natural key is `merge-add-probe`'s own, `(target_sha, branch_sha)`. That can be a new kind beside it or a second field on `MergeProbeResult`. `read_json` treats a deserialize failure as a miss, so adding a field silently invalidates every existing `merge-add-probe` entry, where a new kind leaves them intact; both self-heal within one cold run. Warm runs then spawn no extra subprocess at all, exactly as `diff-stats` behaves today. The `diff-stats` entries for `(main, branch)` pairs would go unused and age out through the 5000-entry LRU.\n\n## Against it\n\nThe number moves for ordinary branches too, not only squash-merged ones. Merging resolves the branch against main's newer content, so wherever main has touched the same regions the totals shift by a few lines.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Measured across one repo's branches</summary>\n\n| ahead / behind | three-dot | merge-add |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 / 40 | +77 -5 | +77 -5 |\n| 1 / 4 | +379 -47 | +382 -47 |\n| 3 / 6 | +627 -51 | +662 -46 |\n| 4 / 4 | +1564 -281 | +1571 -280 |\n| 1 / 4 (squash-merged) | +305 -42 | +0 -0 |\n\nBeing behind is not what moves it; the branch 40 commits behind is identical. Overlap is.\n\n</details>\n\nIt also doesn't cover every squash merge. When `merge-tree` conflicts there is no tree to diff, and those rows fall back to the three-dot number. Some are `✗`, but `patch_id_match` rows are integrated precisely because `merge-tree` conflicted, which is the squash merge where main later touched the same files. So the one integration reason reached through a conflict keeps its inflated count.\n\nTwo smaller costs. `main…±` is named for the three-dot form and preview tab 3 shows that diff, so both would want renaming. And if `[list] columns` narrows to `main…±` without `Status`, `merge-tree` becomes newly required, where `BranchDiff` needs only its own task today.\n\n## Smaller alternative\n\nDim or blank the count cells when `main_state` is integrated. It is known before render and already dims the rest of the row, so extending it to the counts needs no new git work and leaves ordinary branches' numbers untouched. It also covers every integration reason, `patch_id_match` included, which the merge-tree approach cannot. That settles the contradiction without deciding what the column should mean.\n\n#3280 moved these columns onto the upstream-aware base \"the integration column already uses\". This is the same idea one step further: taking that check's result, not only its base.\n\nMeasurements are from macOS, on a 259-commit repo and on git.git.\n\n> _This was written by Claude Code on behalf of max-sixty_\n","comments":[{"id":"IC_kwDOQEkLn88AAAABPRwPyQ","author":{"login":"worktrunk-bot"},"authorAssociation":"COLLABORATOR","body":"Four things from the code that bear on the choice — including one gap in the merge-tree option beyond the two already listed.\n\n**On `⊂` rows the proposed number is zero by construction, not by measurement.** `would_merge_add` is literally `tree != target_tree` ([`would_merge_add_to_target`](https://github.com/max-sixty/worktrunk/blob/d73ce691581467332d6afd1ed29e3a5f0f438b9b/src/git/repository/integration.rs#L355-L363)), so wherever the symbol resolves through a clean `merge-tree` the tree-to-tree diff is necessarily empty — confirmed on a scratch squash-merge repo, where `merge-tree --write-tree` returned a tree SHA byte-identical to `main^{tree}`. Across the five reasons: `Ancestor` and `NoAddedChanges` already render blank (three-dot is empty by definition); `TreesMatch` and `MergeAddsNothing` would render `+0 -0`, which the boolean beside them already determines; `PatchIdMatch` keeps today's inflated number. So on the rows this issue is about, the merge-tree option *is* \"blank the cells where merge-tree is clean\" — a strict subset of the smaller alternative — and the tree diff's real yield is the shifted counts on non-integrated overlapping branches, i.e. the \"Against it\" rows.\n\n**A second gap beside patch-id: a diverged local/upstream default.** `WouldMergeAddTask` probes the primary target and, when the two have diverged, the [secondary as well](https://github.com/max-sixty/worktrunk/blob/d73ce691581467332d6afd1ed29e3a5f0f438b9b/src/commands/list/collect/tasks.rs#L451-L463) — a branch merged into either side reads as integrated. `BranchDiffTask` measures against [`comparison_base()`](https://github.com/max-sixty/worktrunk/blob/d73ce691581467332d6afd1ed29e3a5f0f438b9b/src/commands/list/collect/tasks.rs#L530-L549), which is [`IntegrationTargets::primary` only](https://github.com/max-sixty/worktrunk/blob/d73ce691581467332d6afd1ed29e3a5f0f438b9b/src/git/repository/integration.rs#L36-L41). A branch integrated via the secondary therefore keeps `⊂` while its merge against the primary is non-empty, so the contradiction survives there too — \"the column and the symbol beside it would be reading the same thing\" holds exactly when `secondary` is `None`.\n\n**Redefining the column redefines `main.diff` in JSON.** One `BranchDiffTotals` feeds both the cell and the JSON field, in v1 ([`json_output.rs`](https://github.com/max-sixty/worktrunk/blob/d73ce691581467332d6afd1ed29e3a5f0f438b9b/src/commands/list/json_output.rs#L354-L358)) and v2 ([`json_v2.rs`](https://github.com/max-sixty/worktrunk/blob/d73ce691581467332d6afd1ed29e3a5f0f438b9b/src/commands/list/json_v2.rs#L819)), where the schema describes it as \"Lines added/deleted vs the default branch\". Consumers already receive `main_state` and `integration_reason` alongside it and can reconcile the two themselves, so the machine surface has no contradiction to fix — the merge-tree option would be a silent semantic change under a versioned schema, while dim/blank leaves it untouched.\n\n**Sparse checkout is a carry-over cost.** The three-dot path appends the sparse pathspec to the diff and bypasses both cache layers while sparse is active ([`diff.rs`](https://github.com/max-sixty/worktrunk/blob/d73ce691581467332d6afd1ed29e3a5f0f438b9b/src/git/repository/diff.rs#L374-L379)). The `merge-add-probe` key has no sparse component — correctly, since its boolean is repo-wide — so hanging the `LineDiff` off that key needs the guard added there, otherwise a sparse checkout serves numbers from a cache that ignores the filter that produced them.\n\n<details><summary>Scratch repro of the zero-by-construction claim</summary>\n\n```bash\ngit init -q -b main . && printf 'a\\nb\\nc\\n' > f.txt && printf 'x\\n' > other.txt\ngit add -A && git commit -qm init\ngit checkout -qb feature && printf 'a\\nb\\nc\\nd\\ne\\n' > f.txt && git commit -qam \"feature adds lines\"\ngit checkout -q main && git merge -q --squash feature && git commit -qm \"squash merge feature\"\nprintf 'x\\ny\\n' > other.txt && git commit -qam \"unrelated main work\"\n\ngit diff --shortstat main...feature\n#  1 file changed, 2 insertions(+)\n\ngit merge-tree --write-tree main feature   # 33161d3ecc8adb59d7601e737a4a693ab304f044\ngit rev-parse main^{tree}                  # 33161d3ecc8adb59d7601e737a4a693ab304f044\ngit diff --shortstat 33161d3 33161d3       # (empty)\n```\n\nThe two SHAs are equal because that equality *is* the integration check — the same comparison `would_merge_add_to_target` makes to decide `⊂`. No separate diff is needed to know the result is `+0 -0`.\n\n</details>\n","createdAt":"2026-08-17T21:03:23Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/max-sixty/worktrunk/issues/3842#issuecomment-5320216521","viewerDidAuthor":false}],"title":"main…± reports a full diff for branches ⊂ says are already integrated","url":"https://github.com/max-sixty/worktrunk/issues/3842"}
{"body":"Thank you for this nice project.\nI have a suggestion: `wt cleanup` or something like this should automatically remove all merged branches && worktrees","comments":[{"id":"IC_kwDOQEkLn88AAAABFKnXDQ","author":{"login":"worktrunk-bot"},"authorAssociation":"COLLABORATOR","body":"Thanks for the suggestion! This already exists as [`wt step prune`](https://worktrunk.dev/step/#wt-step-prune), which bulk-removes worktrees and branches that have been integrated into the default branch — using the same integration criteria as `wt remove`.\n\nIt covers all three shapes:\n\n- live worktrees whose branch is merged → worktree removed and branch deleted\n- stale worktree entries (directory already gone) → pruned and branch deleted\n- merged branches with no worktree at all → branch deleted\n\nA few useful flags:\n\n```console\nwt step prune --dry-run     # preview what would be removed\nwt step prune               # remove all merged worktrees & branches\nwt step prune --min-age=0s  # drop the default 1-day age guard\n```\n\nThe `--min-age` guard (default 1 day) exists so a worktree you just created off the default branch isn't removed — it looks \"merged\" because its branch still points at the same commit. In `wt list`, candidates show `_` (same commit) or `⊂` (content integrated).\n\nIf that's not quite what you had in mind — for example if you wanted it to operate without the integration check, or scoped differently — could you clarify the additional behavior you're after? A maintainer will take a look either way.\n","createdAt":"2026-06-07T06:21:24Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/max-sixty/worktrunk/issues/3004#issuecomment-4641642253","viewerDidAuthor":false},{"id":"IC_kwDOQEkLn88AAAABFkOQzg","author":{"login":"leotsarev"},"authorAssociation":"NONE","body":"Thanks, that's exactly that I have in mind! But I think that discoverability of this feature have a room for improvement. I expected something like `wt cleanup` or `wt prune` or `wt remove --all`. I tried `wt --help` and `wt remove --help`. Kind of hard to guess that I need `wt step --help` to discover it.","createdAt":"2026-06-10T09:13:13Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/max-sixty/worktrunk/issues/3004#issuecomment-4668494030","viewerDidAuthor":false},{"id":"IC_kwDOQEkLn88AAAABFkPvdw","author":{"login":"worktrunk-bot"},"authorAssociation":"COLLABORATOR","body":"That's fair — `wt step` is a less obvious home for it, and `wt prune` / `wt remove --all` are reasonable first guesses.\n\nIn the meantime, you can give yourself the exact name you reached for with an alias:\n\n```toml\n[aliases]\nprune = \"wt step prune {{ args }}\"\n```\n\n`wt <name>` resolves a built-in first and then an alias, and there's no built-in `prune`, so `wt prune` (and `wt prune --dry-run`, `wt prune --min-age=0s`, …) will forward straight through. Drop that in your user config (`~/.config/wt/wt.toml`) and it's available across every repo. See the [aliases docs](https://worktrunk.dev/extending/#aliases) for the syntax.\n\nOn the native side — adding a top-level `wt prune`, or surfacing the pointer from `wt remove --help` — I'll leave that for a maintainer to weigh, since it's a change to the CLI surface. Thanks for the clear feedback on where you looked first; that's the useful part.\n","createdAt":"2026-06-10T09:16:21Z","includesCreatedEdit":false,"isMinimized":false,"minimizedReason":"","reactionGroups":[],"url":"https://github.com/max-sixty/worktrunk/issues/3004#issuecomment-4668518263","viewerDidAuthor":false}],"title":"Command to remove all merged branches","url":"https://github.com/max-sixty/worktrunk/issues/3004"}
